        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_582"/>
        <dc:title>A lightweight decision-level fusion model for pig disease identification using multi-modal data</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>In intensive pig farming, highly contagious diseases such as African swine fever, blue ear disease, transmissible gastroenteritis, and swine dysentery pose increasing threats to the pig industry. Timely detection of sick pigs and accurate diagnosis of their diseases are crucial for saving livestock. Therefore, this study integrates pigs’ excretion behavior and fecal characteristics through a model decision layer and proposes the HCC-YOLOv8-LSTM model for pig health monitoring. First, the YOLOv8s model is improved by introducing the High Performance GPU Network (HGNet) module for feature extraction and fusion, which reduces computational costs and enhances feature extraction capabilities. The Cross-Stage Partial Convolution (CSPC) module, with Partial Convolution at its core, reduces the number of parameters and computations while effectively extracting spatial features, thereby enhancing the model’s ability to identify abnormal pigs. Cross-Scale Feature Fusion (CSFF), as the Neck layer, fuses features of adjacent scales, enhancing the model’s adaptability to health characteristics of pigs of different scales. Then, we introduce the Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) network to extract timecontinuous excretion behavior characteristics of pigs. By considering both excretion behavior and excretion characteristics through a decision layer fusion approach, the issue of poor accuracy due to single-mode features is reduced. The model achieves an mAP of 0.951 and a recall of 0.971. This lightweight model can be deployed on mobile devices, fits the practical conditions of pig farms, and can be applied to unmanned noncontact real-time detection systems to monitor pigs’ health through their excretion behaviors and characteristics.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>04/2025</dc:date>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0168169925000420</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
